---
import ProviderPlaybookTemplate from '../components/ProviderPlaybookTemplate.astro';
const data = {
 "kind": "nameservers",
 "name": "AWS Route 53",
 "title": "How to Change Nameservers in AWS Route 53",
 "description": "Change nameservers in AWS Route 53 step by step: where the setting lives, what values to enter, and the propagation window to expect.",
 "intro": "Nameserver changes are delegation changes: you're telling the internet which servers hold your domain's records. Here is exactly where AWS Route 53 keeps that setting and what to expect.",
 "panel": "Route 53 gives the zone's four NS nameservers on the hosted zone page. To USE Route 53, your registrar's nameservers must be set to that NS set. To LEAVE Route 53, update the NS record inside the zone if your new host also manages it, and repoint the registrar.",
 "steps": [
  [
   "Copy the NS set",
   "Hosted zone details: copy all four nameservers."
  ],
  [
   "Update the registrar",
   "Set those four as custom nameservers wherever the domain is registered."
  ],
  [
   "Wait and verify",
   "Delegation updates within the hour typically; confirm with an NS lookup, then watch propagation."
  ]
 ],
 "quirk": "Route 53 hosted zones cost about $0.50/month each (plus query charges) - the only DNS host in our playbook that bills for hosting. Also: NS and SOA records are managed records; don't delete them.",
 "faqs": [
  [
   "Why is Route 53 paid when others are free?",
   "It's an AWS infrastructure service with SLAs and global anycast; pricing is per zone and per query, integrated into your AWS bill."
  ],
  [
   "How long do DNS changes take in AWS Route 53?",
   "The provider serves new values quickly, but resolvers keep old answers until each record's TTL expires. Verify live values with the record lookup, then watch the propagation checker until resolvers agree."
  ],
  [
   "Where do I find AWS Route 53's nameservers?",
   "This playbook's nameservers guide covers the exact location. They are the delegation pair your registrar must point at for AWS Route 53 to be authoritative."
  ],
  [
   "Should I lower TTL before changing records in AWS Route 53?",
   "Yes, for anything planned: set TTL to 300 seconds at least one old-TTL cycle ahead, make the change, confirm propagation, then restore the TTL."
  ],
  [
   "Can I keep email with one provider and DNS in AWS Route 53?",
   "Yes: DNS host and mail provider are independent. Publish the mail provider's MX, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC records in AWS Route 53, and keep those records unproxied."
  ],
  [
   "Which records should every domain have?",
   "A (and ideally AAAA) for the website, MX plus SPF/DKIM/DMARC for mail, and CAA to restrict certificate issuance. Everything else is per-service."
  ]
 ],
 "related": [
  [
   "DNS record types reference",
   "/dns-records/"
  ],
  [
   "TTL explained",
   "/what-is-ttl-in-dns/"
  ]
 ]
};
---
<ProviderPlaybookTemplate data={data} />
